{"results": [{"id": "hp_doc_d7169a7d15f4fc6590eb7bc79d1374b16460b9f83a086dd0e1393f6ae7c7c2e0", "title": "Diane Middlebrook", "text": "Diane Helen Wood Middlebrook (April 16, 1939 – December 15, 2007) was an American biographer, poet, and teacher. She taught feminist studies for many years at Stanford University. She is best known for critically acclaimed biographies of poets Anne Sexton and Sylvia Plath (along with Plath's husband Ted Hughes), and jazz musician Billy Tipton. Middlebrook was preparing a biography of the Roman poet Ovid, to be published in 2008. Her death brought that project to a close.", "score": 19.6146}, {"id": "hp_doc_05e09c3aa0dd4a5b2aef00b278c29ef0e1f9b6415b58dc4376673aae11a96230", "title": "Billy Tipton", "text": "William Lee Tipton (December 29, 1914 – January 21, 1989) was an American jazz musician and bandleader. He is also notable for the postmortem discovery that, although he lived his adult life as a man, he was assigned female at birth.", "score": 19.5961}, {"id": "hp_doc_a5e7051573307321f6e7d7dd4b6812622f6e7ca0f5d46552d676312ff0ddd168", "title": "The Born Losers", "text": "Born Losers is a 1968 action film and the first of the Billy Jack movies. The film introduced Tom Laughlin as the half-Indian Green Beret Vietnam veteran Billy Jack. Since 1954 Laughlin had been trying to produce his \"Billy Jack\" script about discrimination toward American Indians. In 1968 he decided to introduce the Billy Jack character in a quickly written script designed to capitalize on the then-popular trend in motorcycle gang movies. The story was based on a real incident from 1964 where members of the Hells Angels were arrested for raping five teenage girls in Monterey, California.", "score": 9.0737}], "remaining_searches": 1}
